{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '3\\\\.1|ws-project|projection|section 5|Section 5' docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '360,535p'; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md | sed -n '580,655p'\"","aggregated_output":"68:  its projection (3.1), a UDP socket adapter and its deposit medium (3.2),\n272:projection closed it (3.1), the pair adapter closed it on an `in` gap or\n320:### 3.1 WebSocket\n323:a channel is one of its attachments plus a projection. **Reused**: the\n332:**New, the projection.** The mirror and the link speak raw request and\n333:answer maps, so each channel end composes one step, `ws-project`, between\n336:`ws-project` holds the reading cursor on the attachment's traffic medium,\n341:`:ws/transport-error`) makes `ws-project` close the ring buffer: channel\n342:loss is then the link's `end` observation (2.4). `ws-project` is a step the\n479:`ws-project` and then `mirror-step` over the projected reader and the\n602:(section 5) and the five `dao.stream.md` amendments (section 2), which add\n   360\t  <channel identity> :dao.stream.udp/host h :dao.stream.udp/port p}`.\n   361\t  `attach!` binds or reuses a local socket and returns a handle whose writer\n   362\t  path is datagrams to that address.\n   363\t- One CBOR value per datagram, canonical profile. The budget covers the\n   364\t  whole encoded datagram, fragment envelope included: at most 1200 bytes,\n   365\t  the figure `dao.jing.dht.node` already uses to clear common path MTUs.\n   366\t- **Fragmentation is channel-internal.** An encoded message over budget is\n   367\t  split into `k` datagrams, each\n   368\t  `{:dao.stream.remote/id n :dao.stream.udp/part i :dao.stream.udp/parts k\n   369\t  :dao.stream.udp/bytes b}`, where `n` is the carried message's own request\n   370\t  id. The receiver reassembles by `[channel attachment identity,\n   371\t  source-address, direction, id]`, because ids are unique only per channel\n   372\t  reader and one socket may serve several attachments, and delivers only a\n   373\t  complete message. Loss of any part is loss of the whole message,\n   374\t  recovered by the link's resend rule. Reassembly is bounded by two\n   375\t  composition data, `:dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes` (default 64 KiB)\n   376\t  and `:dao.stream.udp/max-partial-messages`, evicting the oldest partial\n   377\t  message; no clock. Neither the mirror step nor the link sees a fragment.\n   378\t- Replies go to the datagram's source address, never to an address claimed\n   379\t  in a payload.\n   380\t- A send may name an explicit destination from an already-bound socket. Hole\n   381\t  punching depends on it (section 4).\n   382\t- Unordered and best-effort. Attachment identity is the remote host and port\n   383\t  as the socket saw them; it is the reflexive address a meeting peer\n   384\t  republishes.\n   385\t\n   386\tHosts: clj (`DatagramSocket`), cljs on Node (`dgram`), cljd\n   387\t(`RawDatagramSocket`). Browsers have no UDP.\n   388\t\n   389\t### 3.3 Pair\n   390\t\n   391\tA pair descriptor names two remote descriptors:\n   392\t\n   393\t```clojure\n   394\t{:dao.stream/type      :dao.stream/pair\n   395\t :dao.stream/identity  <channel identity>\n   396\t :dao.stream.remote/in  <remote descriptor>    ; this peer reads here\n   397\t :dao.stream.remote/out <remote descriptor>}   ; this peer writes here\n   398\t```\n   399\t\n   400\t`attach!` on it returns a channel whose reader is a reflection of `in` and\n   401\twhose writer is a reflection of `out`. When a third peer serves both\n   402\tstreams from its table, two peers that cannot reach each other have a\n   403\tchannel, and the third peer runs nothing but mirror steps over two ring\n   404\tbuffers; the requests and answers inside are values in its streams it never\n   405\tinterprets. Streams are values sent through streams. Ordered; best-effort\n   406\tunder the inboxes' retention. **An `in` gap ends the channel.** The pair\n   407\tadapter holds the reading cursor on `in`; when `next` there answers `gap`,\n   408\tframes were lost and the adapter cannot say which, so it closes the channel\n   409\treader for this link. The link observes `end` and runs its channel-loss\n   410\tpath (2.4): outstanding ids are abandoned, not re-sent, each abandoned\n   411\t`append!` is reported `append-unknown`, and reattachment is the caller's,\n   412\tby `attach!` on the same pair descriptor, which mints a fresh reading\n   413\tcursor on `in` at `:newest`. The gap is never reported to an inner reader\n   414\tas a source's `gap`. Double framing consumes budget: an inner value inside\n   415\tan outer `append!` must fit the outer channel's budget with both envelopes.\n   416\t\n   417\t## 4. Reachability and NAT\n   418\t\n   419\tA channel needs one end that can receive an unsolicited packet, or a\n   420\tcoordinated simultaneous send. The protocol cannot change that; it makes\n   421\tthe workarounds conventions. One peer, M below, is reachable and serves two\n   422\tordinary streams from its table; which peer that is carries no protocol\n   423\tmeaning, and any reachable peer can take its place.\n   424\t\n   425\t- One side receives unsolicited (public address, forwarded port, or\n   426\t  endpoint-independent mapping and filtering): direct; the other side dials,\n   427\t  WebSocket or UDP.\n   428\t- Endpoint-dependent mapping or filtering on one or both sides, punching\n   429\t  still possible: hole punch through M (below), UDP allowed.\n   430\t- Endpoint-dependent mapping and filtering on both sides (a symmetric NAT;\n   431\t  the strict carrier-grade paths): punching is unreliable; relay through a\n   432\t  pair served by M.\n   433\t- UDP blocked: WebSocket only, to a reachable peer, and the browser rule\n   434\t  applies; without one, impossible (section 1).\n   435\t- Browser at one end: it dials, and two browsers relay through a pair. A\n   436\t  WebRTC data channel is a future channel type whose candidates ride the\n   437\t  same meeting stream.\n   438\t\n   439\t**Hole punching, as a convention.** M serves a `meet-requests` stream with\n   440\tsurface `#{:writer}` and a `meet-board` stream with surface `#{:reader}`,\n   441\tboth ordinary table entries. Over a UDP channel a peer appends\n   442\t`{:meet/here <name>}`; M's interpreter reads the request and the channel\n   443\tattachment identity it arrived on, which for UDP is the reflexive address,\n   444\tand appends `{:meet/seen <name> :meet/reflexive {:host h :port p}\n   445\t:dao.lease/lease L}` to the board. Both parties of a call read the board,\n   446\tthen each sends a `descriptor` request with a fresh id to the other's\n   447\treflexive address from the same socket M observed. A path exists when an\n   448\tanswer, even a `not-found` error, carrying one of this peer's outstanding\n   449\tids arrives from the expected address; the first inbound datagram alone\n   450\tconfirms nothing. That is the whole of STUN, as facts on a stream; no probe\n   451\tframe exists.\n   452\t\n   453\t**Bounded meeting work.** M's interpreter bounds its own work: the board's\n   454\tretention (composition data, evict-oldest by default), the number of active\n   455\tpairs in its table, and the fanout of one driver step. A meeting request M\n   456\tcannot honor within its bounds is refused by a gate on its `meet-requests`\n   457\tentry and observed by the asker as `:dao.stream/refused` through its\n   458\treflection: exhaustion is a present answer, not silence. A peer reachable\n   459\tthrough M is reachable only while its channel to M lives, so its keep-alive\n   460\tcadence is bounded by the NAT binding's lifetime, tens of seconds for UDP,\n   461\tlonger for TCP.\n   462\t\n   463\t**Relay, as a convention.** A peer asks M, through `meet-requests`, for a\n   464\tpair; M creates two ring buffers, enters them in its table under a lease,\n   465\tand posts their remote descriptors on the board; the asking peers attach a\n   466\tpair channel over them (3.3). M interprets nothing it relays, and that two\n   467\tunreachable peers need M at all is a fact about the network, not a role in\n   468\tthis protocol. Reflexive addresses on a board are readable by every reader\n   469\tof the board; this convention claims no private delivery.\n   470\t\n   471\t## 5. Conventions that are not protocol\n   472\t\n   473\tNone of these has a frame. Each is peers using sections 2 and 3.\n   474\t\n   475\t**The toy.** Peer B holds a handle `s` over the single value `\"hello\"`,\n   476\tidentity `\"str-1\"`, complete history, surface `#{:reader}`. B's table is\n   477\t`{\"str-1\" {:handle s :surface #{:reader}}}`. B accepts WebSocket connections\n   478\tas `dao.stream.ws.md` specifies and drives, per accepted connection,\n   479\t`ws-project` and then `mirror-step` over the projected reader and the\n   480\tsocket handle. B publishes `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/remote\n   481\t:dao.stream/identity \"str-1\" :dao.stream/channel <B's ws descriptor>}`.\n   482\tPeer A calls `attach!`; the link dials and sends the `descriptor` probe;\n   483\tthe answer carries `:dao.stream.remote/surface #{:reader}`. A calls\n   484\t`cursor` with `:oldest`: no answer filed, the link sends the request and\n   485\tanswers `transport-error` with `:dao.stream/retry? true`; on the next call\n   486\tthe drain files the source's `ok` with `c0`. `next c0` proceeds the same\n   487\tway: `blocked`, then `ok \"hello\" c1`, and `next c1` is `end`. Nothing knew\n   488\tit was a string.\n   489\t\n   490\t**Request and response service.** A peer S enters `requests` with surface\n   491\t`#{:writer}` and `answers` with surface `#{:reader}` in its table and runs\n   492\tan interpreter over the local ends; a peer P attaches reflections to both,\n   493\tmints `:newest` on `answers` first, appends `{:svc/request r ...}` through\n   494\t`requests`, and reads `answers` until it observes `:svc/request r`. Request\n   495\tids are self-minted random values so callers sharing one `answers` stream\n   496\tdo not collide; a caller that needs private answers asks S for a pair. S is\n   497\ta \"server\" only in that its table has those entries and its interpreter has\n   498\tthat policy, and either peer may serve such a pair to the other over the\n   499\tsame channel. This is Linda's request medium, and the shape `yin.repl` and\n   500\tcontent lookup take (implementation plan, section 1). The meeting board and\n   501\tthe relay pair are section 4's instances of it.\n   502\t\n   503\t**Peer names.** A name is a self-certifying hash of a public key, the\n   504\tkickoff-hash form of `dao.stream.discovery.md`. It appears only inside\n   505\tconvention payloads. Nothing authenticates it until authentication exists.\n   506\t\n   507\t## 6. Lease integration\n   508\t\n   509\tA served stream is a network resource, so its lifetime beyond one call is\n   510\tgoverned by `dao.lease.md`. The judge is composed inside the peer that\n   511\tpossesses the resource, as that contract requires. What a lease governs:\n   512\t\n   513\t- A table entry a peer serves by its own policy: no lease; the peer retires\n   514\t  it when it likes.\n   515\t- A table entry served for a remote party (a stream a migrated continuation\n   516\t  still reads): yes; the remote party holds it, the holding peer grants and\n   517\t  judges, the identity is the subject.\n   518\t- A relay pair on a meeting peer: yes; the requesting peer holds it, the\n   519\t  meeting peer grants and judges, the two identities are the subject.\n   520\t- Link state, UDP reassembly buffers, channel sockets: no; governed by close\n   521\t  or by a bounded buffer.\n   522\t- NAT mappings and punch state: no; possessed by the NAT box, so no judge\n   523\t  can exist, and keep-alive cadence is the peer's own (section 4).\n   524\t- Board postings: no; records under the board's retention, each carrying the\n   525\t  lease id of the registration or pair it belongs to, and a reader treats a\n   526\t  posting whose lease has a `:lapsed` on the board as stale.\n   527\t\n   528\t**Mechanism: no new wire shape.** A grantor enters two conventional streams\n   529\tin its table, `lease-proposals` with surface `#{:writer}` and\n   530\t`lease-grants` with surface `#{:reader}`, published as remote descriptors\n   531\tbeside its meeting descriptors. A holder appends a proposal through a\n   532\treflection on the first and reads its grant through a reflection on the\n   533\tsecond. The grant creates one more table entry, a ring buffer with surface\n   534\t`#{:writer}`, and names its identity as `:dao.lease/holder`: this is the\n   535\tholder's **renewal medium**, and whatever lands on it is attributed to the\n   580\tvisible. Channel encryption is deferred: a relay sees every envelope and\n   581\tany value not hidden by middleware. A future channel wrapper must order\n   582\tcodec, encryption and fragmentation in that order. Key distribution is\n   583\tunspecified; use library ciphers.\n   584\t\n   585\tThe capability-agnostic seam is an opaque per-request credential key,\n   586\tmirror-supplied channel context, mirror-side gate and reflection-side\n   587\t`present`; refusal is `:dao.stream/refused`. No token or verification\n   588\tpolicy is specified. The gate consumes an index-published decision. The\n   589\tone ShiBi fit this version chooses: its index interpreter publishes the\n   590\ttuple space's current query value as the decision, and its query\n   591\tinterpreter is `verify`, a query over that value with the request as\n   592\tbindings, the shape `dao.space.query/q` has. `dao.shibi.md` states the\n   593\tadapter contract and its limits (one snapshot, bounded cost, no waiting);\n   594\trequest-specific decisions awaited over streams are a non-goal there.\n   595\t\n   596\tLease attribution uses per-author media; renewal gates are optional.\n   597\t\n   598\t## 8. Unification and contract boundary\n   599\t\n   600\t`dao.jing.content` succeeds `dao.jing.remote` whole. The implementation\n   601\tplan lists the successor vocabulary (section 1), the network-path fates\n   602\t(section 5) and the five `dao.stream.md` amendments (section 2), which add\n   603\tno network or capability concept.\n   604\t\n   605\t[impl-plan]: ./dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/1790485743084-vm-engineer-dao-stream-remote-slice3-r2.glm-flash.report.md; nl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md | sed -n '175,225p'; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc | sed -n '400,430p;545,575p'\"","aggregated_output":"# Slice 3 round 2 — ws-project + composition implemented; retirement deferred\n\n## What was implemented\n\n**NEW `src/cljc/dao/stream/ws_project.cljc`** (portable cljc; compiles and runs on all three lanes):\n- `terminal-events` (`ws_project.cljc:34`) — the four channel-ending deposits per 3.1: `:ws/closed`, `:ws/ended`, `:ws/not-found`, `:ws/transport-error`.\n- The projection (3.1's `ws-project` step): `projection` (`:43`) holds the reading cursor on the attachment's traffic medium plus the wired ring; `project!` (`:70`) keeps only events whose `:ws/attachment` names this channel, appends each `:ws/payload`'s `:ws/value` onto the ring (`contains?`-guarded, so nil rides), drops `:ws/error` diagnostics, and on any terminal event closes the ring (`:86`); `step!` (`:96`) reads the medium to blocked, adopts the gap recovery cursor, marks the projection ended on the medium's own `end`; `closed?`/`reading-cursor` accessors.\n- The accepting peer: `make-acceptor` (`:140`), `adopt!` (`:180`, creates the per-connection media via the composition's `:make-media` and acknowledges the offer per `dao.stream.ws.md` Serving with `:ws/command :ws/accept` + `:ws/deposit` + `:ws/admission`), `accept-step!` (`:230`, transport step → offer adoption → second transport step so the accept frame is on the wire before anything is answered → per session projection then `remote/mirror-step` over the projected reader and the socket handle), `sessions` (`:258`), `session-end` (`:266`).\n- The dialing peer: `dial` (`:288`), `dial-attach!` (`:336` — ws attach, projection, then `remote/attacher` over `{channel-descriptor {:reader ring :writer handle}}`; the link's drain then runs inside every reflection operation), `dial-step!` (`:362` — projection then this end's own mirror step, so the dialing end serves the other direction on the same connection), `channel` (`:377`).\n\n**Deferral comments in `src/cljc/dao/stream/ws.cljc`** — comments only, zero behavior change (verified by diff): at `make-endpoint` (`ws.cljc:410-414`, the `:served` table) and at `accept-slot!` (`ws.cljc:554-556`, the wire `{:ws/frame :ws/accept}` send), both naming the slices-4/5 retirement and why it waits (the copy path's consumers).\n\n## Ambiguities resolved (minimal readings)\n1. The traffic medium's own `end` closes the projection but not the channel ring — 3.1 names only the four deposited events as ring-closers (unit-tested as such).\n2. `:ws/payload` without `:ws/value` is dropped as malformed, never appended as nil.\n3. A medium gap adopts the recovery cursor (mirrors `mirror-step`/drain gap handling); a gap is not channel loss.\n4. One `dial` = one active attachment; reattachment composes a fresh dial with a fresh minted cursor (`dao.stream.ws.md`'s one-active-attachment client boundary).\n5. `accept-step!` runs `endpoint-step` twice per tick so the mirror never answers a non-open ws handle (a `full` answer is dropped by `write-answer!`).\n6. The toy's \"complete history\" source is a ring closed by its owner, which is what makes `next c1` answer the source's own `end`.\n\n## Tests\n- `test/dao/stream/ws_project_test.cljc` (portable; ran on all three lanes), 10 tests / 36 assertions: value forwarding in order incl. nil; `:ws/attachment` filtering incl. a foreign terminal event; `:ws/error` diagnostics dropped; cursor keeping across steps; payload-then-terminal in one step; `:ws/closed`/`:ws/ended` close the ring, later steps project nothing, the ring refuses appends; `:ws/not-found`/`:ws/transport-error` close the ring; opened/offer/valueless-payload ignored; gap recovery; medium end vs ring fate.\n- `test/dao/stream/ws_project_jvm_test.clj` (JVM; **real loopback sockets** — http-kit listener + `java.net.http` client), 3 tests / 25 assertions: the section-5 toy end to end (probe confirmation's `:dao.stream.remote/surface #{:reader}` via the link's event writer; `cursor :oldest` → retry then `ok c0`; `next c0` → `ok \"hello\" c1`; `next c1` → `end`); both directions serving on one connection (B reflects `\"str-2\"` over the same accepted session and reads `\"backward\"` through A's dial-side mirror); a closed connection observed as the link's end (close → both projections close → both links answer `transport-error` `:dao.stream.remote/channel-gone`; `descriptor` still `ok`).\n\n## Lane counts (sequential, solo; kondo 0/0 and cljstyle clean on all touched files)\n- JVM `clj -M:test`: **2,268 tests / 183,303 assertions / 0 failures, 0 errors** (run twice; stable). Baseline at HEAD excluding my namespaces: 2,255/183,242 — test count matches your quoted 2,255 exactly; the quoted 183,224 is 18 assertions below HEAD (the slice-2 r2 fix assertions post-date that figure). My delta: +13/+61.\n- Node `bb test:cljs`: **2,177 tests / 49,865 assertions / 0 failures, 0 errors** (baseline 2,167/49,829 → +10/+36 = my portable namespace).\n- Dart `bb test:cljd`: **2,139 passed, all tests passed** (baseline 2,129 → +10).\n\n## What the orchestrator must run\nThe true cross-host socket proofs, which need a remote-protocol peer program per host (the `dao.stream.slice-peer` pattern): compose these same `dao.stream.ws-project` acceptor/dial pieces with `dao.stream.ws.node` and `dao.stream.ws.dart` in a peer namespace, then run **clj→Node** (clj listener serves, Node dials and reads), **Node→clj** (Node's acceptor serves, clj's dial reflects back over the same connection), and **cljd→clj** (clj serves, the Dart peer dials). The namespace is portable and already compiles/runs on the Node and Dart lanes (unit tests above). The browser dials-and-reads lane is also yours: no browser harness exists in the three lanes; the composition is identical with `dao.stream.ws.browser/connect!` as the dial side.\n\nStatus: COMPLETE   175\t  own Close-section \"server-side accepted-connection handle\", track the\n   176\t  contract's wording and are re-worded with the code slices, not before.\n   177\t\n   178\t## 5. Unification: the fate of every existing path\n   179\t\n   180\tMoved from the spec's section 8, which keeps only the normative summary.\n   181\tEvery existing network path, verified against the tree, and its fate:\n   182\t\n   183\t- `dao.stream.ws` and the jvm, node, browser, dart adapters: **subsumed**.\n   184\t  Already a channel: writer plus deposit medium plus the spec's 3.1\n   185\t  projection. Its accept and disclaim frames and served-path table become\n   186\t  unnecessary once `not-found` is an answer per identity; retiring them is\n   187\t  slice 3.\n   188\t- `dao.stream.serving`: **retired**. Forwards a source into every accepted\n   189\t  socket and flattens a source `gap` into a detach: the copy model. Its\n   190\t  consumers are `yin.repl.serve` and `dao.jing.remote` (which requires and\n   191\t  drives it); both are retired by slices 4 and 5.\n   192\t- `dao.stream.forward`: **unrelated**. A local copy step; replication by\n   193\t  convention, not exposure.\n   194\t- `dao.stream.rpc`: **convention-over**. Correlation by id over two streams\n   195\t  is the spec's section 5 service; its state machine may be kept as one\n   196\t  such vocabulary over reflections, or retired in slice 5.\n   197\t- `dao.stream.rpc.ws`: **retired**. Its only job is translating `:ws/`\n   198\t  envelopes for rpc; the 3.1 projection is the channel binding now.\n   199\t- `dao.stream.apply` as a wire envelope: **retired**. Its ok-or-error\n   200\t  wrapper duplicates the outcome map. The `:dao.stream.apply/call` AST node\n   201\t  and the VM's FFI bridge keep the name and are unrelated to the network.\n   202\t- `yin.repl.serve`, `connect`, `driver`, `adapter`, the `daostream:ws://`\n   203\t  URL: **convention-over**. An eval service: `requests` and `answers`\n   204\t  streams, `:op/eval` as payload; the URL becomes a remote descriptor for\n   205\t  that pair. The shared-shell privilege is its interpreter's policy.\n   206\t- `dao.jing.remote`, `remote.step`, `remote.async`, the `:dao.jing/remote`\n   207\t  coordinate in `dao.jing.coordinate`: **retired**. Deprecated whole;\n   208\t  replaced by `dao.jing.content` (section 1).\n   209\t- `yin.repl.link`, `yin.vm.linker` M3 and M4: **convention-over**. The\n   210\t  content pair is a request and response service over reflections or local\n   211\t  ring buffers; the linker's stepped core is unchanged.\n   212\t- `dao.data.btree.storage/hydrate-async`, `store-tree-async`:\n   213\t  **convention-over**. Consume the content client of section 1; the\n   214\t  callback facade is host policy, per `dao.stream.md` OD-5.\n   215\t- `dao.jing.dht.node` UDP DHT: **unrelated**. Raw JVM datagram RPC with its\n   216\t  own Kademlia envelope; its socket rules and 1200-byte budget are the UDP\n   217\t  channel's template.\n   218\t- `dao.stream.transit`, `dao.stream.cbor`: **subsumed**. The channel\n   219\t  codecs, unchanged.\n   400\t                    {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n   401\t                     :dao.stream/handle handle\n   402\t                     :dao.stream/attachment id})\n   403\t                (outcome :dao.stream/transport-error)))\n   404\t            (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _\n   405\t              (outcome :dao.stream/transport-error))))))))\n   406\t\n   407\t\n   408\t;; Server acceptance is intentionally an endpoint composition object, not a\n   409\t;; global transport directory.  Its slots are supplied and owned by the host.\n   410\t;; Deferred retirement (dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md, slice 3,\n   411\t;; round 2): the :served table and the accept/disclaim handshake go once the\n   412\t;; mirror answers not-found per identity; they stay until dao.stream.serving,\n   413\t;; which routes accepted offers by these descriptors, is deleted with its\n   414\t;; consumers in slices 4 and 5.\n   415\t(defn make-endpoint\n   416\t  [{:keys [served control control-admission slots codecs] :as config}]\n   417\t  (let [control-target (checked-target control control-admission)\n   418\t        codecs (or codecs [transit/profile])]\n   419\t    (when-not (and (map? served) (seq slots))\n   420\t      (throw (ex-info \"WebSocket endpoint needs served paths and handoff slots\" {:config config})))\n   421\t    (when-not (and (seq codecs) (every? codec-profile? codecs))\n   422\t      (throw (ex-info \"WebSocket endpoint needs at least one codec profile\" {:codecs codecs})))\n   423\t    (when-not (apply distinct? (map :ws/subprotocol codecs))\n   424\t      (throw (ex-info \"WebSocket endpoint codec subprotocols must be distinct\" {:codecs codecs})))\n   425\t    (when-not (= :portable-values (:value-domain control-admission))\n   426\t      (throw (ex-info \"endpoint control medium must carry portable values\" {:admission control-admission})))\n   427\t    (doseq [[path descriptor] served]\n   428\t      ;; A served descriptor crosses under every profile the endpoint speaks,\n   429\t      ;; or the profile that cannot carry it must not be offered.\n   430\t      (when-not (and (= path (:ws/path descriptor))\n   545\t  [ack attachment]\n   546\t  (and (map? ack)\n   547\t       (= attachment (:ws/attachment ack))\n   548\t       (= :ws/accept (:ws/command ack))\n   549\t       (writer-target? (:ws/deposit ack))\n   550\t       (admission? (:ws/admission ack))))\n   551\t\n   552\t\n   553\t;; Deferred retirement: the wire {:ws/frame :ws/accept} answer to an\n   554\t;; acknowledgement goes with the served-path table and the disclaim frame\n   555\t;; (slices 4 and 5); the copy path's clients still resolve on receiving it.\n   556\t(defn- accept-slot!\n   557\t  [endpoint index slot ack]\n   558\t  (let [hstate (:handle-state slot)\n   559\t        socket (:socket @hstate)\n   560\t        codec (:codec slot)\n   561\t        target (:ws/deposit ack)]\n   562\t    (swap! hstate assoc :deposit target :phase :open :resolution? true)\n   563\t    (let [sent (send-result socket ((:ws/encode codec) {:ws/frame :ws/accept}))]\n   564\t      (when-not (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome sent))\n   565\t        (swap! hstate assoc :phase :closed)\n   566\t        (invoke-close! socket 1011 \"dao.stream/accept-failed\")\n   567\t        (terminal! hstate (:attachment slot) :ws/closed))\n   568\t      ;; Either way the endpoint is done with this connection: the composition\n   569\t      ;; owns an accepted session, and a failed acceptance is already torn down.\n   570\t      (release-slot! endpoint index (:attachment slot)))))\n   571\t\n   572\t\n   573\t(defn endpoint-step\n   574\t  \"Poll each configured acknowledgement slot once, without waiting or\n   575\t   self-scheduling.  Returns the endpoint after retaining its next cursors.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
